# Timber - ten woodwinds, each in its own range, each levelled, and each now
# playing every note of that range.
#
# **Every patch says the range it is played in.** `body` is the instrument's
# lowest note, and the bodies here are the real instruments' bottom notes -
# the seeds had that right. But the `note=` centres had been placed by
# sweeping for "where it makes a pitch", and four of them were *below* the
# instrument's own bottom note, with the audition phrase going another
# octave down from there. Below the body the tube under the fingers clamps
# to nothing and what is left is a bare line and a reed, which is what was
# heard as "breath and no note". So every patch carries `range=lo..hi`, from
# the instrument and not from the model, the phrase stays inside it, and the
# app puts the keyboard there when the patch is loaded.
#
# **The range is the acceptance test, not the centre.** `audition sweep
# Timber` plays every note of every range and says which ones are dead or on
# the wrong partial. All two hundred and twenty pass. The centre alone never
# saw the saxophone's bottom note come out an octave high or the oboe lose
# the top five of its own.
#
# **The reed's table is a curve now, and that is where its gain comes from.**
# A straight table has only one way to make gain - tilt it - but the tilt and
# the resting point are then the same number, so a loop gain of 1.24 meant a
# reed already 97 per cent shut, with three per cent of its travel left
# against wave swings ten times that. The gain was capped at `2 r_rest -
# offset` however hard the player blew: the bassoon took 240 ms to speak,
# `pressure` moved nothing, and the gain the solve asked for rippled away
# with whatever the tube below the fingers was doing, which is what killed
# the oboe above note 74. A cube costs a sixth of the aperture for the same
# gain and raises the ceiling from 2 - offset to 4 - 3 offset. Measured:
# every patch speaks in 30-70 ms where it took 50-240, the solve now gets
# the gain it asks for at every note of every range, and `pressure` is worth
# seven decibels from 0.4 to 1.2 where it used to be worth none.
#
# **The breath is no longer taken through the envelope.** A wind player's
# breath is up before the note sounds - that is what a tongued attack is -
# and running the envelope through the mouthpiece solve meant the loop was
# linearised at a different pressure every block on the way up, so the gain
# crossed one somewhere in the middle of the attack. The envelope shapes
# what leaves the instrument; the breath has its own four-millisecond ramp.
#
# **The lattice is a filter and not a lag.** A row of open holes is a
# cutoff: below it the wave turns round whole. A one-pole is eleven per cent
# down at half its corner, so the top of a range was losing an eighth of its
# loop to holes that should have given it back untouched. Two poles, or four
# when `holes` is deep, flat below and steeper above.
#
# **The walls take a root of the frequency, per round trip.** Stated at a
# fixed frequency - a fifth off above four times the bottom note - it was the
# right law said wrongly: deep enough to stop the saxophone overblowing, it
# took three notes off the top of the oboe. A round trip is as long as the
# tube that is sounding, so the loss per turn goes as the root of the partial
# and as one over the root of the note, and a high note is a short tube that
# loses less. That is also the only thing telling a cone which of its modes
# to sound, and at a third off at the bottom note every patch holds its
# fundamental everywhere.
#
# **`hollow` is the new column and it is the one to voice against.** Even
# harmonics over odd, in decibels: a cylinder is -18 to -26, a cone -3 to
# -12, a stopped pipe -39. It exists because a levelled bank has the same
# rms all the way down and a centroid cannot tell a clarinet from a
# saxophone - the two read within a percent of each other with thirteen
# decibels between their second harmonics. It caught the oboe and the bassoon
# measuring as hollow as clarinets: their embouchures were tight enough to
# keep the reed nearly linear, and a cone with no even harmonics is a cone in
# name only. Loosened to 0.45 and 0.48 they read -3 and -9.
#
# **Embouchure is a real control now** and it trades three things at once, as
# a lip does: looser is louder, richer and flatter. The oboe moved 5 dB, 14
# decibels of second harmonic and 11 cents between 0.7 and 0.45. The flatness
# is the reed's own curve pulling with amplitude, it differs patch by patch
# (+14 cents on Stopped Wood, -3 on the old tight oboe), and `fine` carries
# it. Init sets nothing by rule and so reads 12 cents flat.
#
# Every centre is within 3 cents and every patch within half a decibel of
# -22 dB RMS. Across a whole range the tuning is mostly within 10 cents with
# the odd note at 20, which is the tube below the fingers pulling each
# fingering differently, and 7-11 dB of loudness, which is a wind instrument.
#
# **What is still wrong.** The radiated fundamental sits 14 dB under its own
# partials by construction (`arrive - 0.8 low`), which is backwards for a
# chalumeau. A cone's modes come out 4-7 per cent flat of harmonic, because
# only the fundamental gets a tube length solved for it and every filter in
# the loop has phase the other partials keep - that is what leaves the oboe's
# second harmonic in a trough between modes. The flute's attack is eight
# times the edge of its own tone, which is a chiff and probably right. And
# the timbres past the bore and the embouchure are still as seeded.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Timber` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Timber
# A melody rather than one held note: one note is what one note sounds
# like, and a bank can pass that patch by patch and still be the same
# sound all the way down.
role    tune

# Auditioned at middle C rather than wherever the harness would otherwise put
# it: every other patch here declares its own note and range, and without one
# Init was being measured in a dead spot of the bore - 6 dB down on note 60 for
# the sake of two semitones, which read as a broken house level and was not.
patch Init  note=60

patch Clarinet  note=62 range=52..76
  attack         0.02 s
  breath         0.1
  keys           0.2
  volume         0.80

  fine           11 cents
  volume         0.83
patch "Bass Clarinet"  note=50 range=40..62
  body           73.4 Hz
  breath         0.16
  embouchure     0.5
  keys           0.35
  lattice        1000 Hz
  pressure       0.9
  tongue         0.6
  volume         0.98

  fine           13 cents
  volume         0.98
patch "Alto Sax"  note=60 range=50..74
  attack         0.015 s
  body           138.6 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.2
  embouchure     0.5
  keys           0.3
  lattice        900 Hz
  pressure       0.95
  tongue         0.75
  vibrato        9 cents
  volume         0.58

  fine           9 cents
  volume         0.57
patch Oboe  note=70 range=60..84
  attack         0.012 s
  body           233.1 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.08
  embouchure     0.45
  family         #1
  keys           0.2
  lattice        1600 Hz
  pressure       0.95
  tongue         0.8
  vibrato        12 cents
  volume         0.57

  fine           8 cents
  volume         0.42
patch Bassoon  note=46 range=36..60
  attack         0.025 s
  body           58.3 Hz
  bore           #1
  # 0.45 is where the cone's harmonics come right, but the bassoon plays an
  # octave and a half above its own body and that is where the wall loss has
  # least to say about which mode wins: at 0.45 note 50 came out as two modes
  # beating and no pitch at all. 0.48 keeps the series and settles it.
  embouchure     0.48
  family         #1
  keys           0.4
  lattice        700 Hz
  pressure       0.9
  volume         0.86

  fine           10 cents
  volume         0.86
patch Flute  note=72 range=62..86
  attack         0.04 s
  body           261.6 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.45
  family         #2
  keys           0.15
  lattice        1400 Hz
  pressure       0.8
  tongue         0.35
  vibrato        10.8 cents
  volume         0.62

  volume         0.23
patch "Pan Pipe"  note=67 range=57..79
  aim            0.1
  attack         0.06 s
  body           196 Hz
  breath         0.6
  family         #2
  jet            0.55
  lattice        2200 Hz
  pressure       0.7
  tongue         0.2
  volume         0.55

  volume         0.55
patch "Stopped Wood"  note=55 range=46..68
  answer         0.385
  attack         0.05 s
  body           110 Hz
  breath         0.3
  # Dark from the filter now, not from the lattice. A lattice at 420 Hz on
  # a 110 Hz tube is under four times its bottom note - no real woodwind is
  # under six - and with the deep holes it made no note at all from 58 to
  # 64: measured, +18 and +222 cents at -37 dB, breath and nothing else,
  # which is what Dan heard in the top of the phrase. At 700 Hz with plain
  # holes it plays every note from 46 to 68 within five cents.
  cutoff         4000 Hz
  drive          0.2
  embouchure     0.45
  holes          0.5
  lattice        700 Hz
  pressure       1
  tongue         0.5
  volume         0.97

  fine           14 cents
  volume         0.47
patch Forked  note=62 range=52..74
  # 0.4675, seeded from what shipped, is past the point where the fork
  # stops being a colour and becomes a note the tube cannot hold: it read
  # -4137 cents, which is no pitch at all. It plays at 0.32.
  answer         0.32
  attack         0.04 s
  below          1.74
  bore           #1
  breath         0.25
  embouchure     0.45
  family         #1
  fingering      0.8
  flutter        0.35
  lattice        1200 Hz
  pressure       1
  tongue         0.6
  volume         0.59

  fine           2 cents
  volume         0.27